#!/usr/bin/env bash
# release-tag-check — post-merge, assert that the release this push represents is ACTUALLY
# TAGGED before anything downstream reports the release as shipped. Waits for the tag, then
# refuses if it never arrives.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS (card#6579). `auto-tag-version.yml` and `release-promote-cards.yml` both fire
# on `push: main`, as INDEPENDENT runs with no `needs:` between them. Promote therefore reports
# board cards "Released to main" without ever checking that the tag exists — and on the v0.28.0
# release it did exactly that: auto-tag's push was refused with a 403 at 01:59:46Z, promote
# reported "2 moved" and concluded SUCCESS at 01:59:49Z, and the tag did not exist until a
# human re-ran the job at 02:17:06Z. The board asserted a shipped release for 17m27s while the
# tag was absent, and had the re-run never happened it would have asserted it forever.
#
# THE RACE IS NOT THE FAILURE MODE — IT IS THE NORMAL PATH. Because the two runs start
# together, promote wins on EVERY release, not just when tagging breaks; v0.28.0 was merely the
# run where the tag never arrived to settle it. So this check WAITS rather than refusing
# immediately: an immediate hard-refuse would red every release in the repo.
#
# WHAT THE 403 WAS, AND WHAT THIS DOES NOT CLAIM. The tag push was refused while the job log
# showed `Contents: write` granted, the same token had just fetched successfully, no ruleset or
# tag protection existed, and the identical push succeeded unchanged 17 minutes later. Every
# repo-side cause is excluded by measurement; the refusal came from GitHub's authorization
# service. THIS TOOL DOES NOT FIX THAT AND CANNOT. It closes the SILENCE — the part that
# actually bit — by making an untagged release loud on the surface an operator watches instead
# of leaving a false "Released" stamp behind. A recurrence is still a recurrence; it is just no
# longer quiet.
#
# Usage:
#   release-tag-check --before <ref> --after <ref> [--config PATH] [--remote NAME]
#                     [--timeout SECS] [--interval SECS] [--read-timeout SECS]
#
#   --before   the ref the branch pointed at BEFORE this push (github.event.before)
#   --after    the pushed commit (github.sha) — the tag must point HERE
#   --config   per-repo release config (default `.release-pr.json`), inside this repository
#   --remote   remote to poll (default `origin`)
#   --timeout  seconds to wait for the tag before refusing (default 300; 0 = look once)
#   --interval seconds between polls (default 10; must be >= 1)
#   --read-timeout  seconds ONE `git ls-remote` may take before it is killed and scored as an
#              unreadable remote (default 30; must be >= 1; ignored where `timeout` is absent)
#
# THE RELEASE CLASSIFICATION IS NOT RE-DERIVED HERE. It is delegated to
# `release-artifacts-check --classify-only`, which owns the repo's one definition of "this
# range is a release" (the fork-point version against head's, by VALUE, including the arm that
# re-decides an equal classifying value). A second rule here would
# be free to drift from the gate that guards release PRs.
#
# NEITHER IS THE TAG NAME (card#7203). Which tag a version maps to is `.release-pr.json`'s
# `tag_format`, and its one reader is `release-pr-body`, so this asks that sibling for the
# answer (`--tag`) rather than carrying a second copy of the mapping. It carried one until
# card#7203 — a hardcoded `TAG="v${VERSION}"` — which is correct only for the default scheme:
# a consumer whose config says `{{version}}` or `release-{{version}}` (documented in
# `docs/INSTALL.md` §4 for .NET and date tag schemes) had this tool polling for a tag that
# CANNOT EXIST, so it waited out its whole bound and refused EVERY release — the
# hard-refuse-every-release outcome the wait above exists to avoid, delivered by a detail.
#
# THE NAIVE ALTERNATIVE — "a tag must point at every merge on the release branch" — REFUSES ANY
# PUSH WHOSE TIP IS UNTAGGED, so what it would cost is a question about `main`'s own history,
# and that history moves. RE-DERIVE THE NUMBER, never quote one:
#
#     git tag -l 'v*' | while read -r t; do git rev-parse "$t^{commit}"; done | sort -u > /tmp/tagged
#     comm -23 <(git rev-list --first-parent origin/main | sort) /tmp/tagged | wc -l
#
# Measured at `origin/main` 3cc2d7b (v0.29.0, 2026-08-18): 46 first-parent commits, 42 `v*`
# tags, 4 untagged tips — the root commit and three direct pushes predating the release flow.
# The naive predicate would have refused each of those four as an untagged release. It is
# ALSO true that all 42 first-parent MERGES on `main` are tagged today, so on this history the
# naive rule survives every merge and fails only on direct pushes: it is one docs commit pushed
# to `main` away from redding a run that ships nothing. That is what classification buys, and
# it is a narrower claim than an earlier draft of this header made — "false-alarmed on all six
# non-release merges" reproduced under no denominator on this repo (4 of 46 first-parent
# commits, 0 of 42 first-parent merges, 55 of 97 merges across full ancestry), so it is gone
# rather than restated.
#
# THE POLL IS AGAINST THE REMOTE, NEVER LOCAL REFS, and that is the whole point: the tag is
# created by a DIFFERENT workflow run after this job's checkout was taken, so a local ref set
# is frozen at a moment that by construction predates the tag. `git ls-remote` re-asks the
# server each round. An annotated tag is dereferenced (`^{}`) so it is compared as a commit;
# `auto-tag-version.yml` currently creates a lightweight tag, and this must not depend on that.
#
# THE TIMEOUT IS A COST DECISION, STATED, NOT A CONVERGENCE CLAIM. Default 300s. The automated
# path is fast — auto-tag's entire job, runner pickup through push, measured ~8s on the very
# run that failed — so 300s is roughly 35x observed latency and absorbs runner-queue variance
# with room to spare. It is deliberately NOT sized to the incident's 17m27s: that gap was a
# HUMAN noticing and re-running, and sizing an automated wait to human reaction time would make
# every genuinely-broken release hang for 17 minutes before saying so. AT THE BOUND THIS
# REFUSES AND FAILS LOUDLY rather than waiting longer or passing — stamping "Released" late is
# recoverable, stamping it wrongly is not.
#
# ⇒ WORST-CASE RUNTIME IS STATED HERE, and the three callers that size an outer backstop
# (`timeout-minutes:`) from it point at this block instead of restating it — they carried three
# hand-synced copies and all three were wrong the moment a second bounded read landed:
#
#     classification + --timeout + 2 x --read-timeout
#
# — the classifier's own run, then the wait, then AT MOST TWO further bounded reads: the poll
# that crosses the bound, and the cause-naming second look on the measured-absent branch. That
# second read is why the figure is 2x and not 1x. Where coreutils `timeout` is absent no read is
# bounded at all and the run says so on a `::warning::`, so there is no worst case to state on
# such a host. SIZE THE OUTER BACKSTOP ABOVE THIS, always: the tool's own bound firing first is
# what makes a refused release report `failure`; an outer kill reports `cancelled`, which is not
# a verdict about the tag at all.
#
# A NON-RELEASE PUSH ASSERTS NOTHING and exits 0 saying so. A push whose range cannot be
# classified at all is rc 2 from the classifier and is propagated unchanged — never softened
# into "not a release", because that misclassification is a silent non-run of this whole check,
# which is the exact defect class this tool exists to end.
#
# A READ HAS THREE OUTCOMES. Present, absent, and UNREADABLE are three different facts, and
# the poll below keeps them apart: `git ls-remote`'s status is the gate and its stderr is
# kept, so a remote that cannot be reached is never scored as a tag that is not there. Both
# still REFUSE at the bound — a release that cannot be confirmed must not be reported as
# shipped, so the rc is 1 either way and no caller has to learn a new one — but the messages
# make opposite claims, and only one of them is about the tag.
#
# EVERY REFUSAL NAMES ITS OWN CAUSE, OR SAYS IT COULD NOT MEASURE ONE — the property
# card#6579's review ruled this tool must hold. Since the tag NAME became `tag_format`'s answer
# (above), a measured "no such tag at this commit" no longer implies an untagged release: it
# also happens when `tag_format` and the workflow that creates the tag disagree, and the release
# IS tagged under another name. So that branch takes a SECOND look without the tag-name filter
# before it says anything about a cause. The claim is scoped to the two branches that hold it:
# UNREADABLE REMOTE and ABSENT TAG each claim only what they measured. The THIRD, tag-elsewhere,
# does not: it offers two candidate causes it has not told apart, and that is a known gap.
#
# Exit status: 0 = tag present at the pushed commit, or not a release push. 1 = the release is
# untagged past the bound, the remote could not be READ past the bound (a distinct message
# that asserts nothing about the tag), or the tag exists at a DIFFERENT commit. 2 = usage
# error, or the range could not be classified.
set -euo pipefail
# A bracket range is a COLLATION range and en_US.UTF-8 widens `[0-9]` to non-ASCII digits
# (card#5409); exported so the greps below inherit it, not just this shell.
export LC_ALL=C

die() { echo "release-tag-check: $*" >&2; exit 2; }
command -v git >/dev/null || die "git is required"

# Mirrors kb_require_value in _kb-board-lib.sh. Like its release siblings this script is
# deliberately self-contained and does not source that lib; keep them in sync.
require_value() { [ -n "${2:-}" ] || die "$1 requires a non-empty value"; }
require_uint()  { case "${2:-}" in ''|*[!0-9]*) die "$1 requires a non-negative integer";; esac; }
# --interval takes this one, not require_uint: `--interval 0` is accepted by every
# non-negative test and then polls with no pause at all. Against the ORIGINAL clock — a count
# of intervals — it also never terminated, because WAITED never advanced; the real-elapsed
# clock below ends the wait on time either way, so what is left is an unthrottled
# `ls-remote` flood against the very host this check depends on, for the whole of --timeout.
# Measured on the pre-fix binary: `--timeout 300 --interval 0` never advanced WAITED at all and
# had to be killed from outside (rc 124 under an external 8s timeout, one line of output).
# `--timeout 0` stays legal and means "look once".
require_pint()  { require_uint "$1" "${2:-}"; [ "${2:-0}" -gt 0 ] || die "$1 requires a positive integer — 0 polls without pause"; }

CONFIG=".release-pr.json"; BEFORE=""; AFTER=""; REMOTE="origin"; TIMEOUT=300; INTERVAL=10
READ_BOUND=30
while [ $# -gt 0 ]; do
  case "$1" in
    --before)   require_value "$1" "${2:-}"; BEFORE="$2"; shift 2;;
    --after)    require_value "$1" "${2:-}"; AFTER="$2"; shift 2;;
    --config)   require_value "$1" "${2:-}"; CONFIG="$2"; shift 2;;
    --remote)   require_value "$1" "${2:-}"; REMOTE="$2"; shift 2;;
    --timeout)  require_uint  "$1" "${2:-}"; TIMEOUT="$2"; shift 2;;
    --interval) require_pint  "$1" "${2:-}"; INTERVAL="$2"; shift 2;;
    --read-timeout) require_pint "$1" "${2:-}"; READ_BOUND="$2"; shift 2;;
    # Contiguous leading comment block, not a fixed range — a fixed `sed -n` range is silently
    # wrong in both directions as the header grows or shrinks (card#5145).
    -h|--help) awk 'NR>1 { if (substr($0,1,1) == "#") print; else exit }' "$0"; exit 0;;
    *) die "unknown arg '$1'";;
  esac
done

[ -n "$BEFORE" ] || die "--before <ref> is required"
[ -n "$AFTER" ]  || die "--after <ref> is required"

AFTER_SHA="$(git rev-parse --verify "${AFTER}^{commit}" 2>/dev/null)" \
  || die "cannot resolve --after '$AFTER' to a commit in this clone — the caller must check out with fetch-depth: 0"

# The classifier is the sibling in this same bin/, not whatever a PATH lookup finds: a vendored
# consumer may carry a different toolkit version on PATH, and the two must agree on the rule.
SELF_DIR="$(CDPATH='' cd -- "$(dirname -- "$0")" && pwd)"
CLASSIFIER="$SELF_DIR/release-artifacts-check"
TAGGER="$SELF_DIR/release-pr-body"
# BOTH siblings are checked HERE, before the classification — not each one at its first use.
# The tag reader is only USED on a release push, so checking it there meant a mis-vendored bin/
# ran green on every ordinary push and died rc 2 at the first real release: the highest-cost
# moment, and the exact scenario docs/INSTALL.md §6b's vendoring rule exists for. The cost of
# checking early is one stat() on a push that asserts nothing.
[ -x "$CLASSIFIER" ] || die "$CLASSIFIER is missing or not executable — it owns the release classification this check delegates to"
[ -x "$TAGGER" ] || die "$TAGGER is missing or not executable — it owns .release-pr.json's tag_format, which names the tag this check waits for"

set +e
CLASS_OUT="$("$CLASSIFIER" --classify-only --base "$BEFORE" --head "$AFTER" --config "$CONFIG" 2>&1)"
CLASS_RC=$?
set -e
if [ "$CLASS_RC" -ne 0 ]; then
  # Propagated, never softened. The classifier is fail-closed by design and its rc 2 means "I
  # could not tell" — reading that as "not a release" would silently skip the assertion.
  echo "$CLASS_OUT" >&2
  die "could not classify ${BEFORE}..${AFTER} (release-artifacts-check rc $CLASS_RC) — refusing rather than assuming this is not a release"
fi

VERDICT="$(printf '%s\n' "$CLASS_OUT" | tail -1)"
# THE CLASSIFIER MAY EXPLAIN ITSELF BEFORE ITS VERDICT, and that explanation is relayed rather
# than swallowed: card#6488's arm emits a `::warning::` naming WHICH value it decided a range
# ships when the first regex match is equal at both ends, and that value is the tag this check
# is about to assert. Dropping it would leave the annotation on the PR gate only, never on the
# push that acts on it. The verdict is the LAST line by construction, so the rest is the note.
CLASS_NOTES="$(printf '%s\n' "$CLASS_OUT" | sed '$d')"
[ -z "$CLASS_NOTES" ] || printf '%s\n' "$CLASS_NOTES"
case "$VERDICT" in
  "not-release "*)
    echo "release-tag-check: not a release push (version unchanged across ${BEFORE}..${AFTER}) — no tag asserted"
    exit 0;;
  "release "*) ;;
  *) die "unrecognised classifier verdict '$VERDICT' — refusing rather than guessing";;
esac
VERSION="${VERDICT#release }"

# THE SIBLING IS THE ONE READER OF `tag_format`, and this is the second delegation to a bin in
# this same directory rather than to whatever a PATH lookup finds — same reason as the
# classifier above. `--tag` returns before that tool touches the network, and the VERSION is
# passed explicitly: the classifier may have re-decided which value this range ships (its
# card#6488 arm), and re-reading the version file here would silently assert a different one.
#
# ONE ASYMMETRY, STATED: the classification is read at the FORK POINT (the config a PR cannot
# edit), while `tag_format` is read from the CHECKOUT — which on the push this runs for IS the
# merged commit. That is the right end for this question: the tag being waited for is the one
# the tagging workflow will create from the config as merged, not the one the previous release
# would have named.
# Its stderr is NOT captured — it passes straight through to this tool's own, so the sibling's
# message (`no .release-pr.json in this repo`, a jq failure) reaches the run log in its own
# words. Folding it into the captured value would make it a candidate for the tag name, and
# reading "the last line" back out of a merged stream is the truncating-reader shape this
# repo has a class gate for.
set +e
TAG="$("$TAGGER" --tag --version "$VERSION" --config "$CONFIG")"
TAG_RC=$?
set -e
# FAIL CLOSED, never fall back to `v${VERSION}`. A silent default here would be the hardcode
# this delegation removes, re-minted at the one moment the real answer was unavailable.
if [ "$TAG_RC" -ne 0 ] || [ -z "$TAG" ]; then
  die "could not resolve the tag name for version ${VERSION} from ${CONFIG} (release-pr-body --tag rc ${TAG_RC}, output '${TAG}') — refusing rather than assuming a 'v' prefix this repo may not use"
fi
# THE TAG NAME REACHES A `::error::` ANNOTATION, so a NEWLINE in it is a workflow command this
# tool would be writing on its config's behalf: `"tag_format": "v{{version}}\n::error::…"` put an
# attacker-chosen `::error::` line into the promote job's log. Refused fail-closed, on the same
# terms as the guards above, because no legal tag can be here anyway — `git check-ref-format`
# forbids space and every control character in a ref name, so a name carrying one names nothing
# that can exist and polling for it could only ever refuse the release. The REMOTE side of this
# needs no guard for the same reason: names that come back from `git ls-remote` already passed
# that rule. `printf %q` so the diagnosis can show the offending value without re-emitting it.
case "$TAG" in
  *[[:space:]]*|*[[:cntrl:]]*)
    die "the tag name resolved for version ${VERSION} from ${CONFIG} contains whitespace or a control character ($(printf '%q' "$TAG")) — no such git ref can exist, and a newline here would inject workflow commands into this job's log";;
esac

# EVERY POLL IS BOUNDED, so the tool's own --timeout is what fires first. `release-promote-cards.yml`
# states that ordering — "the tool's own bound fires first and exits 1; a timeout-minutes kill
# reports as `cancelled`, not `failure`" — and nothing enforced it: one `git ls-remote` hanging
# on an unreachable host consumed the whole job entitlement and produced exactly the `cancelled`
# outcome that comment says it avoids. Bounding each read is also what gives the rc
# classification above its input: `timeout` reports 124, which is a READ FAILURE and not an
# absent tag.
#
# `timeout` is PROBED, not assumed (the precedent is `board-session-close`'s advisory runner):
# a BSD/macOS host without coreutils has none, and making it a hard dependency would refuse a
# host on which this check otherwise works. Where it is missing the poll is unbounded and the
# run SAYS SO, because the ordering claim above is then not true on that host — a degraded
# state stated, never a false guarantee kept quiet. GitHub-hosted runners ship coreutils.
LS_BASE=(git ls-remote --tags "$REMOTE")
BOUNDED=0
if command -v timeout >/dev/null 2>&1; then
  BOUNDED=1
  LS_BASE=(timeout "$READ_BOUND" "${LS_BASE[@]}")
else
  echo "::warning::release-tag-check: no \`timeout\` on PATH (coreutils), so each poll of ${REMOTE} is UNBOUNDED. The ${TIMEOUT}s bound below still ends the WAIT, but a single hung read can outlast it on this host." >&2
fi
# The poll asks for ONE tag; the second look below asks for all of them. Both are the same
# bounded read, built once — a second `timeout` prefix assembled at the second call site is how
# one of two copies of a read ends up unbounded.
LS_REMOTE=("${LS_BASE[@]}" "refs/tags/${TAG}" "refs/tags/${TAG}^{}")

# git's stderr is kept, not discarded — it is the only thing that says WHY a read failed, and
# a refusal that cannot name its own cause is what card#6579's review caught here.
ERRF="$(mktemp "${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/release-tag-check.XXXXXX")" || die "cannot create a temporary file for the remote's error output"
trap 'rm -f "$ERRF"' EXIT

# _read_fault <rc> — git's own reason for a failed read, collapsed to ONE line (a GitHub
# annotation keeps only the first) and never left empty. ONE owner for both readers below — the
# poll and the cause-naming second look — because a second copy of a refusal's wording is how
# the very defect this round fixed survived: a message corrected on one branch of a shape and
# not the other.
_read_fault() {
  local rc="$1" msg
  msg="$(tr '\n' ' ' < "$ERRF" | sed -e 's/  */ /g' -e 's/^ //' -e 's/ $//')"
  if [ "$rc" -eq 124 ] && [ "$BOUNDED" -eq 1 ]; then
    msg="the read was KILLED after ${READ_BOUND}s with no answer from ${REMOTE}${msg:+ (${msg})}"
  elif [ -z "$msg" ]; then
    msg="git ls-remote exited ${rc} and printed nothing"
  fi
  printf '%s' "$msg"
}

# A READ HAS THREE OUTCOMES, NOT TWO (the class `tests/read-outcome-collapse-selftest.sh`
# guards). The first cut of this loop captured `git ls-remote`'s status and then threw it away
# — `FOUND="$(_remote_tag_sha || true)"` — and tested the survivor for emptiness, so "the
# server answered and carries no such tag" and "I could not look" became the same empty string.
# Measured with the tag GENUINELY PRESENT and the remote made unreadable: this tool said
# `v0.28.0 does not exist on <remote> after 4s … The release merged and was NOT tagged — check
# the auto-tag-version workflow run`, and sent the operator to a workflow run that was green,
# with git's own error text discarded by the `2>/dev/null`. REFUSING was right; naming that
# cause was not. The rc is therefore the gate, git's stderr is kept, and an unreadable remote
# gets its own refusal that claims nothing about whether the tag exists.
#
# _remote_tag_sha — sets REMOTE_TAG_SHA and REMOTE_READ_ERR, and returns which outcome it was:
#   0  PRESENT    — REMOTE_TAG_SHA is the commit $TAG points at on the remote
#   1  ABSENT     — the remote ANSWERED and carries no $TAG
#   2  UNREADABLE — the read did not complete; REMOTE_READ_ERR carries git's own message
# An annotated tag yields both `refs/tags/X` (the tag object) and `refs/tags/X^{}` (the commit);
# the dereferenced line is authoritative, so it is preferred whenever present. The two awk
# passes read $out, a string already in memory — they have no third outcome to lose.
_remote_tag_sha() {
  local out deref plain rc=0
  REMOTE_TAG_SHA=""; REMOTE_READ_ERR=""
  out="$("${LS_REMOTE[@]}" 2>"$ERRF")" || rc=$?
  if [ "$rc" -ne 0 ]; then
    REMOTE_READ_ERR="$(_read_fault "$rc")"
    return 2
  fi
  deref="$(printf '%s\n' "$out" | awk -v t="refs/tags/${TAG}^{}" '$2 == t { print $1; exit }')"
  plain="$(printf '%s\n' "$out" | awk -v t="refs/tags/${TAG}"    '$2 == t { print $1; exit }')"
  REMOTE_TAG_SHA="${deref:-$plain}"
  [ -n "$REMOTE_TAG_SHA" ] || return 1
  return 0
}

# _tags_at_commit — THE SECOND LOOK, taken only on the measured-absent refusal below, and
# WITHOUT the tag-name filter: which tag NAMES (if any) does ${REMOTE} carry at ${AFTER_SHA}?
# It exists because the refusal must name its own cause and, since the tag name became
# `tag_format`'s answer (card#7203), "no ${TAG} at this commit" has TWO worlds behind it — the
# release was never tagged, or it was tagged under a name this check was not waiting for
# because `tag_format` and the workflow that creates the tag disagree. The poll cannot tell them
# apart: it asks for one refspec.
#
#   0  ANSWERED    — TAGS_AT_COMMIT is the (possibly EMPTY) space-separated set of names, and
#                    empty is a measured "no tag of any name", not a failed look
#   2  UNREADABLE  — TAGS_READ_ERR carries git's own message; the cause stays unmeasured
# The `^{}` spelling is stripped and names deduped: an annotated tag stores the tag object's sha
# on `refs/tags/X` and the commit's on `refs/tags/X^{}`, and the question is about names. A tag
# name cannot contain `^` (git-check-ref-format), so trimming at it cannot truncate a real name.
_tags_at_commit() {
  local out rc=0
  TAGS_AT_COMMIT=""; TAGS_READ_ERR=""
  out="$("${LS_BASE[@]}" 2>"$ERRF")" || rc=$?
  if [ "$rc" -ne 0 ]; then
    TAGS_READ_ERR="$(_read_fault "$rc")"
    return 2
  fi
  TAGS_AT_COMMIT="$(printf '%s\n' "$out" \
    | awk -v s="$AFTER_SHA" '$1 == s { n=$2; sub(/^refs\/tags\//, "", n); sub(/\^.*/, "", n); print n }' \
    | sort -u | tr '\n' ' ' | sed 's/ $//')"
  return 0
}

echo "release-tag-check: release push — ${TAG} must exist at ${AFTER_SHA} (waiting up to ${TIMEOUT}s, polling ${REMOTE} every ${INTERVAL}s)"

# WAITED IS REAL ELAPSED TIME, not a count of intervals. Counting `INTERVAL` per round measures
# the sleeps and nothing else, so the reads themselves — up to READ_BOUND seconds each — fell
# outside the "waiting up to ${TIMEOUT}s" the line above promises, and the promote job's
# `timeout-minutes` backstop could be reached while WAITED still read well under the bound.
# With the elapsed clock the stated bound is the bound: the loop ends within TIMEOUT plus at
# most one final read.
START=$SECONDS
WAITED=0
POLLS=0
UNREADABLE=0
LAST_RC=0
LAST_ERR=""
while :; do
  POLLS=$((POLLS + 1))
  set +e; _remote_tag_sha; LAST_RC=$?; set -e
  WAITED=$((SECONDS - START))
  case "$LAST_RC" in
    0)
      if [ "$REMOTE_TAG_SHA" = "$AFTER_SHA" ]; then
        # THE PREDICATE IS "DID WE LOOK MORE THAN ONCE", not "is the clock above zero". Since
        # WAITED became real elapsed time, a single round-trip that happens to cross a second
        # boundary would otherwise report "(after 1s)" for a tag that was there when we first
        # looked — making a waited-for result and an immediate one indistinguishable in the
        # log, and any assertion on "after" vacuous. (`${WAITED:+…}` is wrong for the same
        # reason twice over: WAITED=0 is a non-empty string.)
        if [ "$POLLS" -gt 1 ]; then
          echo "release-tag-check: ok — ${TAG} present at ${AFTER_SHA} (after ${WAITED}s, ${POLLS} polls)"
        else
          echo "release-tag-check: ok — ${TAG} present at ${AFTER_SHA}"
        fi
        exit 0
      fi
      # Present but elsewhere: auto-tag's own case 3 (two merges claimed one version). Waiting
      # cannot resolve it — a tag does not move on its own — so this refuses immediately.
      echo "::error::release-tag-check: ${TAG} exists on ${REMOTE} at ${REMOTE_TAG_SHA}, but this push is ${AFTER_SHA}. Two merges have claimed the same version, or the release PR did not bump the version file. REFUSING to report this release as shipped." >&2
      exit 1;;
    1) ;;   # ABSENT — the remote ANSWERED. This is the normal state for the first seconds of
            # every release, and it is the outcome the wait exists for; keep waiting.
    2)
      # KEEP POLLING. An unreadable remote is the one outcome that is plausibly transient, and
      # the tag being absent right now is the NORMAL state at this point in a release — so a
      # read failure ends the wait no earlier than an absence does. What it must never do is
      # be counted as one, which is why it is tallied and its message kept.
      UNREADABLE=$((UNREADABLE + 1))
      LAST_ERR="$REMOTE_READ_ERR"
      echo "release-tag-check: could not read ${REMOTE} (poll ${POLLS}, ${WAITED}s in): ${LAST_ERR} — retrying; this is NOT evidence that ${TAG} is absent" >&2;;
  esac
  [ "$WAITED" -lt "$TIMEOUT" ] || break
  sleep "$INTERVAL"
done

# THE BOUND IS REACHED IN TWO DIFFERENT WORLDS, AND THEY GET DIFFERENT REFUSALS. Both refuse —
# the promote job must not report a release it cannot confirm — but only one of them knows
# anything about the tag.
if [ "$LAST_RC" -eq 2 ]; then
  # The LAST poll never answered, so the verdict at the bound has measured nothing about ${TAG}.
  # The wording is scoped to that poll deliberately: an EARLIER poll may well have answered
  # (absence is the normal state early on), and "the remote was never reached" would then be
  # its own false claim — the defect this branch exists to remove, re-minted one line lower.
  # Naming the auto-tag workflow would be wrong-but-specific in the same way: it sends the
  # operator to a run that is very likely green while the actual failure is the read.
  echo "::error::release-tag-check: could NOT READ ${REMOTE} on the FINAL poll after ${TIMEOUT}s (${UNREADABLE} of ${POLLS} poll(s) failed, including the last). Last error: ${LAST_ERR}. This says NOTHING about whether ${TAG} exists: the last look never reached ${REMOTE}, so at the bound the tag is UNMEASURED, not absent. Check network/credentials for ${REMOTE} and re-run; do not read this as an untagged release. REFUSING to report this release as shipped." >&2
  exit 1
fi

# The final poll ANSWERED, so the absence below is measured. Earlier failed reads are still
# reported rather than dropped: they are why the wait had fewer real measurements than polls.
MIXED=""
[ "$UNREADABLE" -eq 0 ] || MIXED=" (${UNREADABLE} of ${POLLS} poll(s) could not read ${REMOTE} — last: ${LAST_ERR} — but the FINAL poll answered, so this absence is measured, not inferred)"

# THE CAUSE IS MEASURED, NOT ASSUMED (card#7203). "The release merged and was NOT tagged" was
# this branch's only wording, and the delegation above created a second world in which it is
# FALSE: with `tag_format` and the tagging workflow disagreeing, the release IS tagged and this
# check waited for a name nobody creates — so the operator was sent to a workflow run that ran
# and succeeded. That is the same defect the UNREADABLE branch above exists to remove and the
# comment at the poll records having removed once already (card#6579's review ruling: a refusal
# NAMES ITS OWN CAUSE, OR CLAIMS NOTHING); it survived on this branch because the branch was
# right when it was written and the delegation moved the world under it. The second look is what
# tells the two apart, and it has the same three outcomes as every other read here — so where it
# cannot be taken, the refusal says the cause is UNMEASURED rather than picking one.
set +e; _tags_at_commit; TAGS_RC=$?; set -e
if [ "$TAGS_RC" -ne 0 ]; then
  CAUSE="The follow-up look that would tell an untagged release from a tag-name mismatch could NOT READ ${REMOTE} (${TAGS_READ_ERR}), so the CAUSE here is unmeasured: check BOTH the workflow that creates your tags for this commit AND how ${CONFIG} resolved version ${VERSION} to ${TAG} (its tag_format if that key is set, else the default v{{version}}) against what that workflow actually creates."
elif [ -n "$TAGS_AT_COMMIT" ]; then
  # Deliberately NOT phrased as "tagged under another name": if ${TAG} itself arrived in the
  # window between the final poll and this look, every clause below is still true.
  CAUSE="A second look WITHOUT the tag-name filter finds ${AFTER_SHA} carrying: ${TAGS_AT_COMMIT}. So this commit IS tagged on ${REMOTE}, and ${TAG} is what ${CONFIG} resolved version ${VERSION} to — its tag_format if that key is set, else the default v{{version}}. That resolution tells THIS CHECK what to expect and does NOT change what your tagging workflow creates, so check those two against each other before reading this as a tagging failure."
else
  CAUSE="No tag of ANY name points at ${AFTER_SHA} on ${REMOTE}, so a tag under a different name is excluded BY MEASUREMENT: the release merged and was NOT tagged — check the auto-tag-version workflow run for this commit; a 403 on its tag push has happened before (card#6579)."
fi
echo "::error::release-tag-check: ${TAG} does not exist on ${REMOTE} after ${TIMEOUT}s, but ${AFTER_SHA} is a release push (version ${VERSION})${MIXED}. ${CAUSE} REFUSING to report this release as shipped." >&2
exit 1
